W44-228a — per-stratum optimal Tier2Knobs lookup table
========================================================

Date: 2026-05-22
Script: benchmarks/sweeps/w44-219-densify/analysis/scripts/w44_228a_per_stratum_knobs.py
Corpus: /mnt/tower/output/zenjxl-tuning/2026-05-22/w44-219-densify/merged.parquet
        (9065 rows; 9018 zenjxl-strategy rows after NaN/inf filter)
TSV:    per_stratum_optima.tsv (8 rows = 8 standard strata)
Log:    w44_228a_run.log

Methodology
-----------
1. Load W44-219 densified corpus; restrict to strategy=zenjxl;
   compute per-image residuals on (ssim2, log_bytes); decode params_blob
   to p1..p6.
2. Fit joint GradientBoostingRegressor (n_estimators=300, max_depth=4,
   learning_rate=0.05, subsample=0.8, seed=42) on
   (p1..p6, effort, distance, 12 image features) → ssim2_resid,
   log_bytes_resid. Identical configuration to W44-221/222/227.
3. Sample anchors at 1 per (content_class × dist_band × effort) cell,
   seed=42; 40 anchors total.
4. Build the 7^5 = 16807-point Tier2Knobs grid using the verbatim
   W44-222 5-knob expander (knob ranges per docs/TIER_2_KNOBS.md).
5. For each of the 8 strata `(content_class × dist_band)`:
   a. Compute the full-param Pareto front on (encoded_bytes, ssim2)
      over 255 unique full-param rows in the corpus.
   b. Predict every knob candidate × stratum-anchor and compute the
      asymmetric coverage (full-Pareto → nearest knob).
   c. Record `max_gap_at_default_knobs` = the gap if every encode is
      pinned at Tier2Knobs::default() (the W44-222 baseline).
   d. Grid-search the 16807 candidates; pick the one with minimum
      `max_pct_gap` (tie-break by minimum `mean_pct_gap`).
   e. Compute `delta_pp = max_gap_default - max_gap_optimum`.

Metric interpretation (IMPORTANT — differs from W44-222 baseline)
------------------------------------------------------------------
The "default-knob max gap" measured here PINS every encode at the
W44-222 default knob settings and asks "how far is the worst stratum
Pareto point from optimum?" This is a stricter metric than W44-222's
"5-knob max gap" (which measures: "given the BEST candidate in the
entire 16807-point knob grid, how far is the worst stratum Pareto
point from optimum?").

Both metrics are legitimate but answer different questions:
- W44-222 "5-knob max gap" answers: *Does the knob space CONTAIN
  good operating points?*
- W44-228a "default-knob max gap" answers: *Are the SHIPPED defaults
  good operating points?*

W44-222 measured the former at 1.15% (PASS the 2pp gate). W44-228a
measures the latter at 49.67% (FAIL → per-stratum defaults can help).

Sanity cross-check with W44-227 density sweep:
- W44-227 screen/very_high 5-knob 7^5 max_pct_gap = 1.147%
  (best-over-grid metric, matches W44-222)
- W44-228a screen/very_high default-knob max_pct_gap = 49.668%
  (default-pinned metric)
- These reconcile: the knob space CAN cover screen/very_high well,
  but the default knobs DON'T sit at the per-stratum optimum for
  screen/very_high.

